{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03647","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03647","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.03647","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dani","familyName":"Roytburg","name":"Roytburg, Dani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Bozoukov","name":"Bozoukov, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jou","familyName":"Barzdukas","name":"Barzdukas, Jou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narmeen","familyName":"Oozeer","name":"Oozeer, Narmeen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breaking the Mirror: Activation-Based Mitigation of Self-Preference in LLM Evaluators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T18:52:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:03:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-22T18:38:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:05:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T22:52:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:24:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) increasingly serve as automated evaluators, yet they suffer from \"self-preference bias\": a tendency to favor their own outputs over those of other models. This bias undermines fairness and reliability in evaluation pipelines, particularly for tasks like preference tuning and model routing. We investigate whether lightweight steering vectors can mitigate this problem at inference time without retraining. We introduce a curated dataset that distinguishes self-preference bias into justified examples of self-preference and unjustified examples of self-preference, and we construct steering vectors using two methods: Contrastive Activation Addition (CAA) and an optimization-based approach. Our results show that steering vectors can reduce unjustified self-preference bias by up to 97\\%, substantially outperforming prompting and direct preference optimization baselines. Yet steering vectors are unstable on legitimate self-preference and unbiased agreement, implying self-preference spans multiple or nonlinear directions. This underscores both their promise and limits as safeguards for LLM-as-judges and motivates more robust interventions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Presented at {Mechanistic Interpretability, Evaluations, Reliable-ML} Workshops, NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03647","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:28:07Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:28:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:15:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03740","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03740","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.03740","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taha","familyName":"Koleilat","name":"Koleilat, Taha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Rivaz","name":"Rivaz, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CLIP-SVD: Efficient and Interpretable Vision-Language Adaptation via Singular Values"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T22:00:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:09:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T23:58:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:13:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-22T16:33:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-23T01:03:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language models (VLMs) like CLIP have shown impressive zero-shot and few-shot learning capabilities across diverse applications. However, adapting these models to new fine-grained domains remains difficult due to reliance on prompt engineering and the high cost of full model fine-tuning. Existing adaptation approaches rely on augmented components, such as prompt tokens and adapter modules, which could limit adaptation quality, destabilize the model, and compromise the rich knowledge learned during pretraining. In this work, we present CLIP-SVD, a multi-modal and parameter-efficient adaptation framework that applies Singular Value Fine-tuning (SVF) to CLIP, leveraging Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) to modify the internal parameter space of CLIP without injecting additional modules. Specifically, we fine-tune only the singular values of the CLIP parameter matrices to rescale the basis vectors for domain adaptation while retaining the pretrained model. This design enables enhanced adaptation performance using only 0.04% of the model's total parameters and better preservation of its generalization ability. CLIP-SVD achieves state-of-the-art classification results on 11 natural and 10 biomedical datasets, outperforming previous methods in both accuracy and generalization under few-shot settings. Additionally, we leverage a natural language-based approach to analyze the effectiveness and dynamics of the CLIP adaptation to allow interpretability of CLIP-SVD. Overall, this work provides the first extensive empirical evaluation of SVD-based finetuning in the vision-language model setting. The code and biomedical corpus are publicly available at https://github.com/HealthX-Lab/CLIP-SVD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"TMLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03740","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:30:22Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:30:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-23T02:21:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03581","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03581","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.03581","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Paglieri","name":"Paglieri, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bartłomiej","familyName":"Cupiał","name":"Cupiał, Bartłomiej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Cook","name":"Cook, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulyana","familyName":"Piterbarg","name":"Piterbarg, Ulyana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jens","familyName":"Tuyls","name":"Tuyls, Jens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward","familyName":"Grefenstette","name":"Grefenstette, Edward","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakob Nicolaus","familyName":"Foerster","name":"Foerster, Jakob Nicolaus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack","familyName":"Parker-Holder","name":"Parker-Holder, Jack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Rocktäschel","name":"Rocktäschel, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning When to Plan: Efficiently Allocating Test-Time Compute for LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T18:00:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:01:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-30T09:12:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:47:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-17T14:21:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-18T01:41:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training large language models (LLMs) to reason via reinforcement learning (RL) significantly improves their problem-solving capabilities. In agentic settings, existing methods like ReAct prompt LLMs to explicitly plan before every action; however, we demonstrate that always planning is computationally expensive and degrades performance on long-horizon tasks, while never planning further limits performance. To address this, we introduce a conceptual framework formalizing dynamic planning for LLM agents, enabling them to flexibly decide when to allocate test-time compute for planning. We propose a simple two-stage training pipeline: (1) supervised fine-tuning on diverse synthetic data to prime models for dynamic planning, and (2) RL to refine this capability in long-horizon environments. Experiments on the Crafter environment show that dynamic planning agents trained with this approach are more sample-efficient and consistently achieve more complex objectives. Additionally, we demonstrate that these agents can be effectively steered by human-written plans, surpassing their independent capabilities and highlighting the potential for safer and more collaborative agentic systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03581","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:26:30Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:26:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-18T02:56:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03888","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03888","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.03888","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeming","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zeming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Muhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"False Sense of Security: Why Probing-based Malicious Input Detection Fails to Generalize"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T05:15:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:19:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-20T16:13:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-21T01:49:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T16:08:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:42:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) can comply with harmful instructions, raising serious safety concerns despite their impressive capabilities. Recent work has leveraged probing-based approaches to study the separability of malicious and benign inputs in LLMs' internal representations, and researchers have proposed using such probing methods for safety detection. We systematically re-examine this paradigm. Motivated by poor out-of-distribution performance, we hypothesize that probes learn superficial patterns rather than semantic harmfulness. Through controlled experiments, we confirm this hypothesis and identify the specific patterns learned: instructional patterns and trigger words. Our investigation follows a systematic approach, progressing from demonstrating comparable performance of simple n-gram methods, to controlled experiments with semantically cleaned datasets, to detailed analysis of pattern dependencies. These results reveal a false sense of security around current probing-based approaches and highlight the need to redesign both models and evaluation protocols, for which we provide further discussions in the hope of suggesting responsible further research in this direction. We have open-sourced the project at https://github.com/WangCheng0116/Why-Probe-Fails."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03888","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:33:57Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:33:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:48:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03646","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03646","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.03646","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haozhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qixin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Che","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Che","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Junhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangzhen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Fangzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wenhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emergent Hierarchical Reasoning in LLMs through Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T18:52:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-08T11:03:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-09T01:13:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-27T17:07:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:42:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning (RL) has proven highly effective at enhancing the complex reasoning abilities of Large Language Models (LLMs), yet underlying mechanisms driving this success remain largely opaque. Our analysis reveals that puzzling phenomena like ``aha moments\", ``length-scaling'' and entropy dynamics are not disparate occurrences but hallmarks of an emergent reasoning hierarchy, akin to the separation of high-level strategic planning from low-level procedural execution in human cognition. We uncover a compelling two-phase dynamic: initially, a model is constrained by procedural correctness and must improve its low-level skills. The learning bottleneck then decisively shifts, with performance gains being driven by the exploration and mastery of high-level strategic planning. This insight exposes a core inefficiency in prevailing RL algorithms like GRPO, which apply optimization pressure agnostically and dilute the learning signal across all tokens. To address this, we propose Hierarchy-Aware Credit Assignment (HICRA), an algorithm that concentrates optimization efforts on high-impact planning tokens. Our extensive experiments validate that HICRA significantly outperforms strong baselines, and offer deep insights into how reasoning advances through the lens of strategic exploration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03646","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:28:05Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:28:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:13:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03746","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03746","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.03746","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anushya","familyName":"Subbiah","name":"Subbiah, Anushya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikram","familyName":"Aggarwal","name":"Aggarwal, Vikram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Pine","name":"Pine, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steffen","familyName":"Rendle","name":"Rendle, Steffen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishna","familyName":"Sayana","name":"Sayana, Krishna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Item ID Generation for Large-Scale LLM-based Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T22:09:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T00:09:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrating product catalogs and user behavior into LLMs can enhance recommendations with broad world knowledge, but the scale of real-world item catalogs, often containing millions of discrete item identifiers (Item IDs), poses a significant challenge. This contrasts with the smaller, tokenized text vocabularies typically used in LLMs. The predominant view within the LLM-based recommendation literature is that it is infeasible to treat item ids as a first class citizen in the LLM and instead some sort of tokenization of an item into multiple tokens is required. However, this creates a key practical bottleneck in serving these models for real-time low-latency applications.\n Our paper challenges this predominant practice and integrates item ids as first class citizens into the LLM. We provide simple, yet highly effective, novel training and inference modifications that enable single-token representations of items and single-step decoding. Our method shows improvements in recommendation quality (Recall and NDCG) over existing techniques on the Amazon shopping datasets while significantly improving inference efficiency by 5x-14x. Our work offers an efficiency perspective distinct from that of other popular approaches within LLM-based recommendation, potentially inspiring further research and opening up a new direction for integrating IDs into LLMs. Our code is available here https://drive.google.com/file/d/1cUMj37rV0Z1bCWMdhQ6i4q4eTRQLURtC"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03746","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-05T01:30:30Z","registered":"2025-09-05T01:30:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-05T01:30:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":6,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=%28doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.03581+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.03646+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.03647+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.03740+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.03746+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.03888%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=10"}}